# CORE DEVELOPMENT STANDARDS

## Context First — No Guesswork
- NEVER write code until you understand the full context
- List all relevant files before making changes
- Read the project's actual dependency/config files (package.json, requirements.txt, composer.json, pom.xml, pubspec.yaml, etc.) before assuming a version, library, or pattern
- Ask specific clarifying questions only when something is genuinely ambiguous or missing
- Follow existing patterns and conventions already in the codebase — don't introduce a new style just because you prefer it

## Challenge Requests — Don't Blindly Follow
- Identify edge cases immediately
- Ask about inputs, outputs, and constraints before implementing
- Question vague or assumed requirements instead of guessing
- If a request implies a significant architectural change, explain the trade-offs and get explicit agreement before making it
- Refine tasks until goals are clear

## Code Quality Standards
- Write modular, testable, clean code
- Add comments and docstrings/JSDoc that explain *why*, not just *what*
- Follow the language/framework's idiomatic conventions and style guide
- Suggest best practices if the current approach is outdated, insecure, or inefficient — but don't silently change direction without flagging it
- No dead code, no commented-out blocks left behind, no unused imports

## Think Bigger Than the File
- Don't just patch — design properly
- Consider maintainability, scalability, and performance, not just "does it run"
- Think across all layers touched: frontend, backend, database/schema, API contracts, UX
- Consider security on every change: input validation, auth/authorization, secrets handling, injection risks
- Plan for user experience, not just functionality

## Debugging Discipline
- Ask for the actual error message, logs, or stack trace before proposing a fix
- Identify the root cause, not just the symptom
- State exactly which file/line needs to change
- Never guess at a fix when the evidence needed to diagnose it is available but unrequested

## Version & Dependency Awareness
- Check the project's actual installed versions before writing code — don't assume "latest" or a remembered version
- Flag deprecated APIs/patterns if the codebase still uses them
- Note breaking changes when suggesting a library or version upgrade
